Ankylosing Spondylitis
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Inflammatory back pain, Morning stiffness improving with exercise, Bilateral sacroiliitis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-006
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of axial inflammatory pain resulting in total immobility and incapacitating muscle spasm.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a young adult suffering from an acute inability to mobilize out of bed due to severe lumbar and pelvic pain. Urgent medical assessment was strictly required to exclude an acute cauda equina syndrome, manage severe refractory nocturnal pain, and administer parenteral analgesia to restore basic functional mobility prior to the next available daytime consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower back and hips are completely locked up; the pain woke me up, and I cannot swing my legs out of bed."
History of Present Illness: Patient under 45 years old reports an insidious onset of inflammatory back pain persisting for over four months. Condition acutely decompensated tonight with extreme nocturnal pain waking the patient at 0300 hours. Reports severe morning-style stiffness in the lower back and pelvis that normally takes hours to resolve, but is currently unrelenting. Baseline pain notably improves with physical activity and worsens with rest. Patient reports alternating buttock pain consistent with active sacroiliitis and recent heel pain suggesting Achilles enthesitis. Currently stranded in bed, unable to load the spine.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound, unable to stand or ambulate.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; unable to access toilet or hydration due to severe axial spasm.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/88 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, diaphoretic, lying supine in severe distress, strictly avoiding truncal movement.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination of lower limbs reveals intact sensation, 5/5 motor strength bilaterally, and normal reflexes. Distinct absence of saddle anesthesia. Significant bilateral sacroiliac joint tenderness on direct palpation and positive FABER testing bilaterally. Exaggerated thoracic kyphosis and loss of normal lumbar lordosis noted. Extreme paraspinal muscle spasm. Schober test demonstrates profoundly restricted lumbar flexion (<2 cm reversal). Bilateral Achilles tendon insertions are thickened and tender. No signs of acutely red, photophobic eye (uveitis absent).
Environmental Observations: Bedroom environment secure, but patient requires physical assistance to achieve a standing posture.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular NSAIDs prescribed; paracetamol ineffective.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute inflammatory flare of Ankylosing Spondylitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out clinically by intact lower limb neurology, normal bilateral reflexes, and absence of saddle anesthesia or bowel/bladder dysfunction.

Acute Lumbar Disc Herniation - Ruled out by negative seated straight leg raise and absence of dermatomal radicular shooting pain.

Nephrolithiasis - Ruled out by absence of costovertebral angle tenderness, colicky pain presentation, and no urinary symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing acute flare of undiagnosed axial spondyloarthropathy, characterized by profound inflammatory back pain, bilateral sacroiliitis, and extreme axial stiffness. The functional consequence was total bedbound immobility in a patient living alone, creating an unsustainable and unsafe clinical scenario. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in unmanaged severe pain, potential dehydration, and further functional decline due to unyielding muscle spasms. Urgent after-hours intervention successfully excluded catastrophic spinal cord pathology, provided immediate high-dose anti-inflammatory treatment, and facilitated safe mobilization.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT. Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 7 days.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Perform gentle pelvic tilts and knee-to-chest stretches in bed before attempting to stand. Consequence: Mechanical lubrication of the inflamed sacroiliac joints, significantly reducing the sharp pain experienced upon initial weight-bearing (negative reinforcement).
Action: Take the prescribed NSAID strictly 30 minutes before the most active part of the day. Consequence: Suppression of systemic inflammation during movement, allowing for a longer, pain-free walking tolerance (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Rheumatology referral required via primary GP.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48-72 hours for HLA-B27 serology and pelvic radiograph orders.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden loss of bowel or bladder control, numbness in the groin area, or rapid development of an acutely painful, red eye with visual changes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly suspected based on clinical criteria but requires formal radiographic and rheumatological confirmation. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute spasm will subside with potent NSAIDs over 48 hours. Long-term management requires targeted biologic therapy and strict daily physiotherapy to prevent permanent spinal fusion.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you lose the ability to control your bladder or bowels, or if your legs become entirely numb and weak.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The injected and oral medications belong to a class of strong anti-inflammatories designed to halt the chemical attack on your spinal joints, reducing swelling and unlocking the muscle spasms.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to spring out of bed. Spend 5 minutes doing gentle knee-to-chest pulls to loosen the joints; this prevents sudden micro-tearing of the stiffened tissues and makes standing vastly less painful.

